DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ccaammccoonnttrrooll utility allows users to access and control the FreeBSD CAM
     subsystem described in cam(4).

     The ccaammccoonnttrrooll utility can cause a loss of data and/or system crashes if
     used improperly.  Even expert users are encouraged to exercise caution
     when using this command.  Novice users should stay away from this util‐
     ity.

     The ccaammccoonnttrrooll utility has a number of primary functions, many of which
     support an optional device identifier.  A device identifier can take one
     of three forms:

     deviceUNIT      Specify a device name and unit number combination, like
                     "da5" or "cd3".

     bus:target      Specify a bus number and target id.  The bus number can
                     be determined from the output of “camcontrol devlist”.
                     The lun defaults to 0.

     bus:target:lun  Specify the bus, target and lun for a device.  (e.g.
                     1:2:0)

     The device identifier, if it is specified, _m_u_s_t come immediately after
     the function name, and before any generic or function-specific arguments.
     Note that the --nn and --uu arguments described below will override any de‐
     vice name or unit number specified beforehand.  The --nn and --uu arguments
     will _n_o_t override a specified bus:target or bus:target:lun, however.

     Most of the ccaammccoonnttrrooll primary functions support these generic arguments:

     --CC _c_o_u_n_t        SCSI command retry count.  In order for this to work, er‐
                     ror recovery (--EE) must be turned on.

     --EE              Instruct the kernel to perform generic SCSI error recov‐
                     ery for the given command.  This is needed in order for
                     the retry count (--CC) to be honored.  Other than retrying
                     commands, the generic error recovery in the code will
                     generally attempt to spin up drives that are not spin‐
                     ning.  It may take some other actions, depending upon the
                     sense code returned from the command.

     --nn _d_e_v___n_a_m_e     Specify the device type to operate on, e.g. "da", "cd".

     --QQ _t_a_s_k___a_t_t_r    SCSI task attribute for the command, if it is a SCSI com‐
                     mand.  This may be ordered, simple, head, or aca.  In
                     most cases this is not needed.  The default is simple,
                     which works with all SCSI devices.  The task attribute
                     may also be specified numerically.

     --tt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t      SCSI command timeout in seconds.  This overrides the de‐
                     fault timeout for any given command.

     --uu _u_n_i_t___n_u_m_b_e_r  Specify the device unit number, e.g. "1", "5".

     --vv              Be verbose, print out sense information for failed SCSI
                     commands.

     Primary command functions:

     ddeevvlliisstt     List all physical devices (logical units) attached to the CAM
                 subsystem.  This also includes a list of peripheral drivers
                 attached to each device.  With the --vv argument, SCSI bus num‐
                 ber, adapter name and unit numbers are printed as well.  On
                 the other hand, with the --bb argument, only the bus adapter,
                 and unit information will be printed, and device information
                 will be omitted.

     ppeerriipphhlliisstt  List all peripheral drivers attached to a given physical de‐
                 vice (logical unit).

     ttuurr         Send the SCSI test unit ready (0x00) command to the given de‐
                 vice.  The ccaammccoonnttrrooll utility will report whether the device
                 is ready or not.

     sseennssee       Send a SCSI REQUEST SENSE command (0x03) to a device.  The
                 decoded sense (or hexdump) is printed to stdout.

                 --DD    Request descriptor sense instead of fixed sense.

                 --xx    Do a hexdump of the returned sense data.

     iinnqquuiirryy     Send a SCSI inquiry command (0x12) to a device.  By default,
                 ccaammccoonnttrrooll will print out the standard inquiry data, device
                 serial number, and transfer rate information.  The user can
                 specify that only certain types of inquiry data be printed:

                 --DD    Get the standard inquiry data.

                 --SS    Print out the serial number.  If this flag is the only
                       one specified, ccaammccoonnttrrooll will not print out "Serial
                       Number" before the value returned by the drive.  This
                       is to aid in script writing.

                 --RR    Print out transfer rate information.

     iiddeennttiiffyy    Send an ATA identify command (0xec) to a device.

     rreeppoorrttlluunnss  Send the SCSI REPORT LUNS (0xA0) command to the given device.
                 By default, ccaammccoonnttrrooll will print out the list of logical
                 units (LUNs) supported by the target device.  There are a
                 couple of options to modify the output:

                 --cc              Just print out a count of LUNs, not the ac‐
                                 tual LUN numbers.

                 --ll              Just print out the LUNs, and do not print out
                                 the count.

                 --rr _r_e_p_o_r_t_t_y_p_e   Specify the type of report to request from
                                 the target:

                                 default    Return the default report.  This
                                            is the ccaammccoonnttrrooll default.  Most
                                            targets will support this report
                                            if they support the REPORT LUNS
                                            command.

                                 wellknown  Return only well known LUNs.

                                 all        Return all available LUNs.

                 ccaammccoonnttrrooll will try to print out LUN numbers in a reasonable
                 format.  It can understand the peripheral, flat, LUN and ex‐
                 tended LUN formats.

     rreeaaddccaapp     Send the SCSI READ CAPACITY command to the given device and
                 display the results.  If the device is larger than 2TB, the
                 SCSI READ CAPACITY (16) service action will be sent to obtain
                 the full size of the device.  By default, ccaammccoonnttrrooll will
                 print out the last logical block of the device, and the
                 blocksize of the device in bytes.  To modify the output for‐
                 mat, use the following options:

                 --bb     Just print out the blocksize, not the last block or
                        device size.  This cannot be used with --NN or --ss.

                 --hh     Print out the device size in human readable (base 2,
                        1K == 1024) format.  This implies --NN and cannot be
                        used with --qq or --bb.

                 --HH     Print out the device size in human readable (base 10,
                        1K == 1000) format.

                 --ll     Skip sending the SCSI READ CAPACITY (10) command.
                        Send only the SCSI READ CAPACITY (16) service action
                        and report its results.  When the two do not match, a
                        quirk is needed to resolve the ambiguity.

                 --NN     Print out the number of blocks in the device instead
                        of the last logical block.

                 --qq     Quiet, print out the numbers only (separated by a
                        comma if --bb or --ss are not specified).

                 --ss     Print out the last logical block or the size of the
                        device only, and omit the blocksize.

                 Note that this command only displays the information, it does
                 not update the kernel data structures.  Use the ccaammccoonnttrrooll
                 reprobe subcommand to do that.

     ssttaarrtt       Send the SCSI Start/Stop Unit (0x1B) command to the given de‐
                 vice with the start bit set.

     ssttoopp        Send the SCSI Start/Stop Unit (0x1B) command to the given de‐
                 vice with the start bit cleared.

     llooaadd        Send the SCSI Start/Stop Unit (0x1B) command to the given de‐
                 vice with the start bit set and the load/eject bit set.

     eejjeecctt       Send the SCSI Start/Stop Unit (0x1B) command to the given de‐
                 vice with the start bit cleared and the load/eject bit set.

